0244: texture cache: visit expired entries by due frame instead of walking the object cache every frame

aurora_end_frame -> gx::texture::end_frame -> sweep_object_caches walked all of
s_textureObjectCaches on the host thread, every frame, after gx::fifo::drain() and immediately
before gfx::finish(). Nothing overlaps it and it waits on nothing, so it is serial CPU time on the
frame critical path: 305 of 2442 main-thread samples (12.5 %) in a `sample` of the playtest build
on a base M1, macOS 26.6, i.e. about 2.3 ms of an 18.2 ms host frame against a 16.7
ms period (interp replay 2.9 + fifo drain 12.9 + this 2.35 + finish 0.4). The table is not small:
the port's [mem] lines report 143 962 to 154 910 texture-object entries (x2_keep_on.log, 60/120/180
s), 48 bytes each.

The walk cannot just be sliced with a cursor. s_textureObjectCaches is an absl::flat_hash_map,
whose iterators are invalidated by any insert that grows the table and by clear(); re-seeking by
index each frame costs the prefix it skips, so past the middle of the table it costs more than the
whole walk; and erase moves a neighbouring element into the freed slot, so even the surviving
elements' positions are not stable. What is stable is the predicate's input: lastUsedFrame only
moves forward, and with s_frameCount incremented just before the sweep, expired() (s_frameCount -
lastUsedFrame > 600) first holds on the frame lastUsedFrame + 601. So each entry is visited when it
is due rather than when it is walked:

* CachedTextureEntry carries the frame its check is due (sweepFrame, in the padding before
  replacementId).
* s_objectCacheSweepQueue is a ring of 1024 due-frame buckets of texObjIds, larger than the
  601-frame deadline, so a ticket always lands on a frame that has not been processed yet. A ticket
  is queued when an entry is created (store_cached_texture, the only insert path) and again by the
  visit itself when the entry turned out to still be in use; one whose id another path already
  evicted, or that is older than the entry now cached under the same id, finds nothing and is
  dropped. The queue is cleared wherever the map is cleared.
* sweep_object_caches swaps out one bucket and runs find + the unchanged expired() predicate
  against the live lastUsedFrame, so an entry is evicted on exactly the frame the every-frame walk
  would have evicted it: retention and the [mem] "texture objects" curve are unchanged, and no
  pixel can change (eviction here is only memory reclamation).

Per-frame work is now proportional to the entries whose idle window expires on that frame (about
260 tickets per frame at 155k entries and a 601-frame window, one find and one comparison each)
instead of to the table size; the pathological case, every entry due at once, costs the old
per-frame price once. The queue holds one u32 per cached entry, about 620 KB at 155k entries.

Verification: `sample` on the same route, texture::end_frame must leave the main-thread stack (305
samples -> ~0) and [perf] host median must drop by about 2.3 ms when present timing is on; the
[mem] "texture objects" curve must stay where it is and still plateau (10 145 / 76 986 / 154 910 at
60/120/180 s). A texture-heavy route walked twice, or the model viewer, is the check that nothing
is kept alive: a ticket that never fires shows up as [mem] texture objects that stop plateauing.

